## ph-greek | The Greeks | philo | 0 | - | -
story 4 | Socrates never wrote a word
- Everything we know of him comes from students, mainly Plato, and critics like Aristophanes.
- His method: ask questions until people discover they don't know what they thought they knew.
- Athens executed him in 399 BC for "corrupting the youth" and impiety. He drank hemlock rather than flee.
> "The unexamined life is not worth living" is from his trial speech

explain 4 | Plato's cave
- Prisoners chained facing a wall see only shadows of objects carried behind them. They think the shadows are reality.
- One escapes, sees the sun, returns, and is mocked by the others.
- *Plato's point: the senses show shadows; reason reveals the real Forms.*
> Aristotle, his student, disagreed

explain 3 | Aristotle: look at the world
- Where Plato put reality in abstract Forms, Aristotle studied actual things: animals, cities, arguments.
- He founded formal logic, classified hundreds of species, and wrote on ethics, politics and physics.
- *Some of his physics was wrong for 2,000 years* (heavy things don't fall faster).
> His ethics is still taught today

explain 4 | Aristotle's virtue: the golden mean
- Courage sits between cowardice and recklessness. Generosity between stinginess and extravagance.
- Virtue is a habit, built by practice, like a skill.
- The goal is eudaimonia: flourishing, not just pleasure.
? What is Aristotle's "golden mean"? :: Virtue lies between two vices of excess and deficiency
> The Stoics took a harder line

quiz 3 | Which Greek philosopher said everything is made of atoms and void, centuries before chemistry?
* Democritus
o Plato
o Pythagoras
o Heraclitus
= Democritus (with his teacher Leucippus), around 400 BC.
= Atomos means "uncuttable".
> Heraclitus said you can't step in the same river twice

story 3 | Diogenes lived in a large jar and mocked Alexander the Great
- Diogenes the Cynic rejected comfort and convention, living in a ceramic jar in Athens.
- Legend: Alexander offered him any wish. Diogenes asked him to stop blocking his sunlight.
- Alexander supposedly said: "If I were not Alexander, I would be Diogenes."
> Next: the Stoics

## ph-stoic | Stoicism | philo | 0 | - | ph-greek
explain 4 | The Stoic dichotomy of control
- Some things are up to us: our judgements, intentions, responses. Most things aren't: other people, the weather, the past.
- Epictetus: suffering comes from treating the second kind as if it were the first.
- *Modern cognitive behavioural therapy borrows this directly.*
> Epictetus was born a slave

story 4 | Marcus Aurelius wrote a diary he never meant anyone to read
- The most powerful man in the Roman world wrote private notes to himself on staying calm, fair and humble.
- We call them the *Meditations*. Many were written on military campaigns.
- "You have power over your mind, not outside events" is a common paraphrase of his ideas.
> Seneca wrote about the shortness of life

quiz 3 | Which Stoic was a former slave?
* Epictetus
o Marcus Aurelius
o Seneca
o Zeno of Citium
= Epictetus was born into slavery in Hierapolis, later freed, and taught in Greece.
= Marcus Aurelius was an emperor; Seneca was a wealthy statesman.
> Stoicism's oddest exercise: imagining loss

explain 3 | Negative visualisation
- Stoics regularly imagined losing the things they valued.
- Not to be gloomy, but to appreciate them now and be prepared.
- *Modern research on gratitude finds something similar.*
> Next: how to decide what's right

## ph-eth | Ethics: three big theories | philo | 0 | - | ph-stoic
explain 3 | Utilitarianism: maximise happiness
- Bentham and Mill: the right act produces the greatest good for the greatest number.
- Consequences are all that matter.
- Problem: it seems to allow terrible acts if the numbers work out.
> Kant says consequences aren't the point

explain 4 | Kant's categorical imperative
- Act only on rules you could will to be universal laws.
- Treat people never merely as means, always also as ends.
- *Lying is wrong even to a murderer at the door, Kant argued.* Many find that conclusion hard to accept.
? Kant's categorical imperative (first formulation)? :: Act only on maxims you could will to become universal laws
> Virtue ethics asks a different question

quiz 3 | Which theory asks "what would a good person do?" rather than "what rule?" or "what outcome?"
* Virtue ethics
o Utilitarianism
o Kantian ethics
o Contractarianism
= Virtue ethics, from Aristotle, focuses on character.
> Peter Singer's drowning child

explain 4 | Singer's drowning child
- You'd ruin your expensive shoes to save a child drowning in front of you.
- A child dying of a preventable disease far away is no less real.
- *So, Singer argued in 1972, failing to donate is morally similar.* It later inspired the effective altruism movement.
> Moral luck: should results matter?

explain 4 | Moral luck
- Two equally careless drivers. One hits a child who runs into the road; the other doesn't.
- We blame the first far more, yet they did exactly the same thing.
- *Thomas Nagel and Bernard Williams named the problem in 1976.*
> Next: do we have free will?

## ph-free | Free will | philo | 0 | - | ph-eth
explain 4 | The free will problem
- If every event is caused by earlier events, so are your choices.
- If some events are random, randomness isn't freedom either.
- *Either way, where does free will fit?*
> Compatibilists think it fits fine

explain 3 | Compatibilism
- Free will doesn't mean uncaused. It means acting from your own reasons without coercion.
- Hume, and today Daniel Dennett, defend this view.
- Most professional philosophers are compatibilists, according to the PhilPapers surveys.
> The Libet experiments are often overstated

myth 4 F | Neuroscience experiments have shown that free will is an illusion.
= Libet (1983) found brain activity rising before people reported deciding to move a finger.
= But later work suggests that signal may be random background fluctuation crossing a threshold, not a hidden decision.
= The experiments don't settle the philosophical question.
> Frankfurt cases: could you have done otherwise?

explain 4 | Frankfurt cases
- A neuroscientist secretly plans to force you to vote for X if you start to choose Y.
- You choose X on your own. The device never activates.
- *You couldn't have done otherwise, yet you seem responsible.* (Harry Frankfurt, 1969)
> Next: what is the mind?

## ph-mind | Philosophy of mind | philo | 0 | - | ph-free
explain 4 | The hard problem of consciousness
- Easy problems: explaining how the brain processes information, sorts inputs, controls behaviour.
- Hard problem (David Chalmers, 1995): why is any of it accompanied by experience?
- *Why does red look like anything at all?*
> Descartes split mind and body

explain 3 | Dualism and the interaction problem
- Descartes: mind and body are different substances.
- Princess Elisabeth of Bohemia asked him in 1643: how can a non-physical mind move a physical body?
- *His answer, via the pineal gland, satisfied almost nobody.*
> What is it like to be a bat?

explain 4 | Nagel's bat
- Bats navigate by echolocation. There's presumably something it is like to be one.
- But no amount of physical knowledge seems to tell you what that is like *from the inside*.
- Thomas Nagel (1974): subjective experience resists objective description.
> Philosophical zombies

explain 4 | Philosophical zombies
- Imagine a being physically identical to you, behaving identically, but with no inner experience.
- If such a being is even conceivable, consciousness might not be purely physical.
- *Critics say it only seems conceivable because we don't understand consciousness yet.*
> Next: what can we know?

## ph-know | Epistemology | philo | 0 | - | ph-mind
explain 3 | Rationalists vs empiricists
- Rationalists (Descartes, Leibniz): some knowledge comes from reason alone.
- Empiricists (Locke, Hume): all knowledge comes from experience. The mind starts as a blank slate.
- Kant tried to combine them: experience gives content, the mind supplies structure.
> Hume's fork

explain 4 | Hume's fork
- Every claim is either a relation of ideas (true by definition, like maths) or a matter of fact (learned by experience).
- Anything that's neither, Hume said, should be "committed to the flames".
- *That targeted much of metaphysics and theology.*
> Brains in vats

explain 4 | Can you prove you're not a brain in a vat?
- Every experience you have could, in principle, be fed to a brain in a vat.
- So how do you know you have hands?
- G. E. Moore's reply: "Here is one hand." He was more certain of that than of any sceptical premise.
> The problem of other minds

quiz 3 | Which philosopher said "I think, therefore I am"?
* Descartes
o Locke
o Kant
o Hume
= "Cogito, ergo sum", from the Discourse on the Method (1637), in French as "je pense, donc je suis".
> Next: logic and its paradoxes

## ph-logic | Logic and paradoxes | philo | 0 | - | ph-know
explain 4 | The liar paradox
- "This sentence is false."
- If it's true, it's false. If it's false, it's true.
- *It's 2,000 years old and still has no universally accepted solution.*
> Russell found the same knot in set theory

story 5 | Russell's paradox broke the foundations of maths
- Consider the set of all sets that don't contain themselves. Does it contain itself?
- Either answer leads to contradiction.
- 1902: Russell wrote to Frege, whose life's work depended on unrestricted sets. Frege replied that arithmetic itself was tottering.
> A barber who shaves everyone who doesn't shave themselves

explain 3 | The sorites paradox
- One grain of sand isn't a heap. Adding one grain never turns a non-heap into a heap.
- So a million grains isn't a heap.
- *Vague words like "tall", "bald" and "heap" resist sharp boundaries.*
> The unexpected hanging

explain 4 | The surprise exam paradox
- A teacher says there'll be a surprise exam next week.
- It can't be Friday (you'd expect it by Thursday night). Then it can't be Thursday either… so none at all?
- The exam comes on Wednesday. Everyone is surprised.
> Logic has a limit, proved by Gödel

story 5! | Gödel proved maths can never prove everything that's true
- 1931: any consistent formal system strong enough for arithmetic contains true statements it can't prove.
- And it can't prove its own consistency.
- *Hilbert's dream of a complete, provably consistent mathematics was dead.*
? Gödel's first incompleteness theorem? :: Any consistent formal system capable of arithmetic has true statements it cannot prove
> Next: existentialism

## ph-exist | Existentialism | philo | 0 | - | ph-logic
explain 4 | "Existence precedes essence"
- Sartre: a paper knife is designed for a purpose before it exists. Humans aren't.
- We exist first, then define ourselves through our choices.
- *So we're "condemned to be free": no excuses, no fixed nature to blame.*
> Camus asked whether life is worth living at all

explain 4 | Camus and the absurd
- We demand meaning; the universe offers none. That clash is "the absurd".
- Sisyphus is condemned to push a boulder uphill forever.
- Camus's conclusion: "One must imagine Sisyphus happy."
> Kierkegaard came first

story 3 | Kierkegaard wrote under a dozen pseudonyms
- The Danish philosopher published as Johannes de Silentio, Victor Eremita, Johannes Climacus and more.
- Each pseudonym argued a different worldview.
- *He wanted readers to choose for themselves, not just follow an author.*
> Nietzsche's eternal return

explain 4 | Nietzsche's eternal recurrence
- Imagine living your life again, exactly the same, infinitely many times.
- Would you curse that thought, or welcome it?
- *Nietzsche's test: live so you'd want it to repeat.*
> Next: politics

## ph-pol | Political philosophy | philo | 0 | - | ph-exist
explain 3 | Hobbes: life without government is "nasty, brutish and short"
- Leviathan (1651): in a state of nature, everyone fears everyone.
- Rational people would hand power to a sovereign in exchange for security.
- He wrote it during and after the English Civil War.
> Locke argued governments must earn their power

explain 3 | Locke's natural rights
- People have rights to life, liberty and property before any government exists.
- Governments exist to protect them; if they don't, people may replace them.
- *Jefferson echoed him in the Declaration of Independence.*
> Rawls's veil of ignorance

explain 5 | Rawls's veil of ignorance
- Design society's rules without knowing who you'll be: rich or poor, healthy or sick, which gender or ethnicity.
- Rawls (1971) argued you'd choose equal basic liberties and allow inequality only if it benefits the worst off.
- *A thought experiment for fairness you can apply to almost anything.*
> Nozick replied with Wilt Chamberlain

explain 4 | Nozick's Wilt Chamberlain argument
- Start with any distribution you think is fair.
- A million fans each freely pay 25 cents to watch Wilt Chamberlain play. He ends up rich.
- *Nozick (1974): if every step was voluntary, how is the result unjust?*
> Next: philosophy from the East

## ph-east | Eastern philosophy | philo | 0 | - | ph-pol
explain 4 | The Buddha's diagnosis
- Four Noble Truths: life involves suffering; suffering comes from craving; it can end; there's a path to ending it.
- The self, he taught, isn't a fixed thing but a changing process (anattā).
> Confucius focused on relationships

explain 3 | Confucius: ethics through roles and ritual
- Social harmony comes from people fulfilling their roles well: parent, child, ruler, friend.
- His version of the golden rule: don't do to others what you wouldn't want done to you.
- *The Analects were compiled by his students after his death.*
> Laozi taught the opposite of striving

explain 3 | The Tao and wu wei
- The Tao Te Ching (traditionally attributed to Laozi): the Way that can be named is not the eternal Way.
- Wu wei: effortless action, like water flowing around obstacles.
- *Water is soft, yet it wears away stone.*
> Zhuangzi dreamed he was a butterfly

story 4 | Zhuangzi's butterfly dream
- Zhuangzi dreamed he was a butterfly, happily fluttering.
- He woke up and wondered: was he Zhuangzi who dreamed of being a butterfly, or a butterfly now dreaming it was Zhuangzi?
- *A Chinese brain-in-a-vat, more than 2,000 years earlier.*
> Next: philosophy of time and physics

## ph-time | Philosophy of time | philo | 0 | - | ph-sci
explain 4 | Does time flow?
- A-theory: there's a real "now" moving through time. Past is gone, future is open.
- B-theory: all times exist equally, like points along a line. "Now" is just where you are.
- *Relativity's lack of absolute simultaneity is often taken to favour the B-theory.*
> McTaggart argued time is unreal

explain 4 | The block universe
- In relativity, observers disagree about what's happening "now" elsewhere.
- If there's no universal now, maybe past, present and future all exist together.
- Einstein, after a friend's death: the distinction between past, present and future is "a stubbornly persistent illusion".
> Can you travel back in time?

explain 4 | The grandfather paradox
- Travel back and stop your grandparents meeting. Then you were never born, so you can't travel back.
- Responses: you'll fail somehow (self-consistency), or you create a new branch of history.
- *General relativity allows some solutions with closed time-like curves.* Whether physics really permits them is unknown.
> Next: philosophy of mathematics

## ph-math | Philosophy of mathematics | philo | 0 | - | ph-time
explain 4 | Is maths discovered or invented?
- Platonists: numbers exist independently, and we discover their properties.
- Formalists: maths is a game of symbols with rules we chose.
- *Why maths fits physics so well is still debated.* Wigner called it "the unreasonable effectiveness of mathematics" (1960).
> Some proofs don't build anything

explain 4 | Constructive vs classical proof
- Classical proof: "There exist irrational a, b with aᵇ rational." Proof: try √2^√2; if it's irrational, raise it to √2.
- You get an answer without knowing which case holds.
- *Constructivists reject such proofs: show me the example.*
> Some infinities are too big to compare

story 4 | The continuum hypothesis can't be settled
- Is there an infinity strictly between the whole numbers and the real numbers?
- Gödel (1940) showed you can't disprove it; Cohen (1963) showed you can't prove it, from standard axioms.
- *It's independent: maths can go either way.*
> Philosophy track complete
